## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of simple rate limiting, memory bounds, and concurrency in a real service.

## Key Concepts

- In-memory data structures for per-key state
- Time-window calculations and pruning
- Concurrency and process-safety; scalability trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
const buckets = new Map();

function limit(req, res, next){
  const key = req.headers['x-api-key'];
  if(!key) return res.status(400).send('API key required');
  const now = Date.now();
  const arr = buckets.get(key) || [];
  // prune
  while (arr.length && now - arr[0] > 10 * 60 * 1000) arr.shift();
  if (arr.length >= 100) return res.status(429).send('Too many requests');
  arr.push(now);
  buckets.set(key, arr);
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a multi-instance deployment?
- What are the limitations of in-memory rate limiting?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical rate-limiting basics, a common reliability concern in public APIs. It tests per-key quotas, in-memory state, and how small designs behave under restart and burst scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window algorithm
- Map-based per-key state
- Time-based pruning
- Testing strategy and limitations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Core limiter (simplified)
const limiter = new Map();

function allowRequest(key, now = Date.now()) {
  const windowMs = 60000;
  const limit = 60;
  const timestamps = limiter.get(key) || [];
  while (timestamps.length && timestamps[0] <= now - windowMs) timestamps.shift();
  if (timestamps.length < limit) {
    timestamps.push(now);
    limiter.set(key, timestamps);
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

